   NAME
	LhA - Create, list, test, and extract LHA/LZH archives.

   FORMAT
	LhA [-<options>] <command> <archive[.LHA|.LZH]> [[homedir]
	     <filespec>...] [@file] [destdir]

   TEMPLATE
	(Classic LhA-style CLI; not AmigaDOS ReadArgs.)
	[-options] COMMAND ARCHIVE [filespec...] [destdir]

   PATH
	C:LhA

   FUNCTION
	LhA is the LhASsA command-line archiver. It uses the same
	command letters and option letters as classic Amiga LhA 2.15,
	so existing scripts and muscle memory continue to work. Archives
	are written and read through the LhASsA LHA engine (.lha / .lzh
	header levels 0-2, CRC16, lh0/lh1/lh5/lh6/lh7 and related
	methods).

	The first argument after any leading options is a single-letter
	(or short multi-letter) <command>. The next argument is the
	archive path (AmigaDOS wildcards allowed except for m). Further
	arguments are filespecs, optional home-directory prefixes, and
	an optional destination directory for extract. Destination
	directories must end with a slash (/) or a colon (:).

	Without other options, LhA reports progress in the classic
	console style (Freezing/Frozen on add; extract/test progress)
	and prints Operation successful. when a command finishes
	cleanly.

	ENV:LHAOPTS is applied at startup as if those options were typed
	after the program name. Use -I to ignore it.

   COMMANDS
	a
	    Add file(s) to an archive (create or append). Existing
	    members with the same name are skipped with a warning
	    (classic behaviour). Use -Qq to skip the duplicate scan.
	    Default stores basenames only; -x (or -r) stores paths.

	c
	    Concatenate / append other archive(s) into the named archive.

	d
	    Delete file(s) from an archive.

	e
	    Extract file(s) using base names only (no stored paths).

	f
	    Freshen file(s) already in the archive (accepted for
	    compatibility; not yet implemented in LhASsA).

	h
	    Hunt for differences between archive and filesystem
	    (accepted; not yet implemented).

	l / lq
	    List archive contents (terse). lq is quiet list.

	m
	    Move file(s) into the archive (accepted; not yet implemented).
	    ARCHIVE must be a single concrete path (no wildcards).

	p
	    Print file(s) from the archive to the current output.

	r
	    Replace file(s) in the archive (accepted; not yet implemented).

	t
	    Test archive integrity (CRC check of stored data).

	u
	    Update file(s) in the archive (accepted; not yet implemented).

	v / vq / vv
	    List archive contents (verbose). vq is quiet verbose list;
	    vv shows full detail.

	x
	    Extract file(s) with full stored path names.

	y
	    Copy archive with new options (accepted; not yet implemented).

   HOME DIRECTORIES
	A filespec ending in / or : is treated as a home directory
	prefix for following filespecs (until the next home or /).
	Stored paths omit the home prefix when -x is active. A lone /
	resets the home to the current directory. -h disables home-
	directory recognition.

   OPTIONS
	Options are case-sensitive and may appear anywhere on the
	command line. A letter followed by 0 disables the option; any
	other digit enables it. A bare letter enables the option.
	Default compression is LHA V2.x-style (-lh5-).

	Implemented / commonly used:

	-q
	    Quiet output (also lq / vq command forms).

	-x
	    Preserve and use path names when adding or extracting.
	    Enabled automatically by -r; use -x0 to store basenames
	    only while still recursing.

	-r
	    Collect action files recursively. Enables -x unless -x0
	    follows. Explicit names are looked up only in the home
	    directory; patterns match in all subdirectories.

	-R
	    Collect archive files recursively for list/extract/test
	    and similar commands.

	-e
	    With -r, archive empty directories as -lhd- members.

	-f
	    Ignore Amiga filenotes (do not store or restore comments).

	-h
	    Disable home-directory recognition.

	-z
	    Store files without compression (lh0).

	-0 / -1 / -2
	    Write header level 0, 1, or 2 when creating entries.

	-2 / -3
	    Use -lh5- / -lh6- compression when adding (default -lh5-).

	-n / -N
	    Suppress byte progress / all progress indicators.

	-X
	    Do not append .lha/.lzh if the archive name has no suffix.

	-y
	    Always append .lha/.lzh.

	-I
	    Ignore ENV:LHAOPTS.

	-Qq
	    Quick add: do not scan for duplicate members before add.

	-Qw
	    Disable AmigaDOS wildcard expansion.

	-Qd
	    Delete autoshow (.displayme) files after showing them.

	w=<dir>
	    Work / extract directory (trailing / or : required for
	    destination directories on extract).

	Many other classic LhA 2.15 switches are parsed for script
	compatibility and are currently no-ops or only partially
	honoured (for example -a, -A, -B, -c, -C, -E, -s, -S, -t, -T,
	-V, -W). Password-protected and self-extracting archives, and
	multivolume (-V / -Qv), are not supported. See the LhASsA
	README for the full compatibility matrix.

   EXAMPLES
	    1> LhA a Backup:Work.lha Work:Sources/#?

	Creates or updates Work.lha, adding matching files (basenames).

	    1> LhA -x a Project.lha Work:MyProject/#?

	Adds matching files with stored path names (volume stripped).

	    1> LhA -r a Src.lha hd:tmp/ *.c *.h

	Recursively adds .c and .h under hd:tmp/, storing paths relative
	to that home directory.

	    1> LhA -R l RAM:#?.lha

	Lists every matching archive under RAM: (recursive collect).

	    1> LhA v Project.lha

	Verbose listing of archive members.

	    1> LhA x Project.lha RAM:Out/

	Extracts into RAM:Out/ with full paths (x). Use e to flatten
	names.

	    1> LhA t Backup:Work.lha

	Tests CRC integrity of every member.

	    1> LhA c All.lha Part1.lha Part2.lha

	Concatenates Part1.lha and Part2.lha into All.lha. Use a to
	add ordinary files; do not use c for that.

   SEE ALSO
	LhX, Load, Handle
